# polycythemia vera — the loop is intact, and the marrow ignores it
#
# This is `erythropoiesis.strombild` with ONE EDGE changed. In the healthy marrow,
# progenitors mature only with EPO: the flow is progenitors × EPO. In polycythemia
# vera the kinase under the EPO receptor, JAK2, carries the V617F mutation and is on
# whether or not EPO is there (Schaltwerk's `jak2-v617f` shows the brake that no
# longer holds, molecule by molecule). At this level that is one deletion: the
# dependency on EPO is gone from the maturation flow. It reads progenitors alone.
#
#     healthy:  progenitors -> red_cells : product(of = progenitors, by = epo, k = 0.00075)
#     PV:       progenitors -> red_cells : proportional(of = progenitors, k = 0.011)
#
# Run it from the healthy steady state and watch what the loop does. The hematocrit
# climbs — past 50, 55, 60 — and the kidney does exactly its job: it sees more oxygen
# and turns EPO DOWN, to a fraction of normal. It is not broken. It is shouting
# "enough" to a marrow that has stopped listening. That is why the diagnostic
# signature of PV is HIGH red-cell mass with LOW EPO: the signal is intact and
# ignored. A low EPO is the loop's fingerprint on the disease.
#
# Where it ends: hematocrit in the mid-sixties here, blood thick enough to clot, which
# is how PV kills — stroke, heart attack, portal vein thrombosis. The standard first
# treatment is PHLEBOTOMY: take blood out, again and again. The event below does that
# at a hematocrit of 55 and shows the sawtooth: it treats the STOCK, not the mechanism,
# so it never stops. A JAK2 inhibitor treats the edge (lower this file's k); that is a
# different kind of intervention and the model can tell them apart.
#
# The OTHER disease of too many red cells is one parameter away, not one edge:
# primary familial polycythemia (PFCP, the truncated receptor with no off switch —
# Schaltwerk's `pfcp`). Its marrow still needs EPO, but gets more signal per EPO. In
# the healthy file, triple the product's k: the hematocrit settles near 52 and EPO near
# 4. Same outcome in words — high red cells, low EPO — different mechanism, and the
# difference is testable: set the kidney's max to 0 in both. PFCP's red cells fall away
# (it still depends on EPO). PV's do not move. Mechanism is what survives the experiment
# that the outcome cannot distinguish.

node epo         : range(0, 400), init(10) @ bucket(fill = water)   # plasma erythropoietin, mU/mL
node progenitors : range(0, 400), init(50) @ bucket(fill = dots)    # erythroid precursors — the V617F clone
node red_cells   : range(0, 80),  init(45) @ bucket(fill = water)   # red-cell mass as hematocrit, %

external kidney
external clearance
external marrow
external apoptosis
external spleen

kidney      -> epo         : repress(of = red_cells, max = 300, k = 35, n = 8)   # the sensor is fine
epo         -> clearance   : proportional(of = epo, k = 3.3)
marrow      -> progenitors : constant(c = 50.4)
progenitors -> apoptosis   : proportional(of = progenitors, k = 1)
progenitors -> red_cells   : proportional(of = progenitors, k = 0.011)           # V617F: maturation no longer reads EPO
red_cells   -> spleen      : proportional(of = red_cells, k = 0.00833)

# the treatment that works on the stock: take blood out when the hematocrit reaches 55
event phlebotomy : red_cells >= 55 : red_cells -= 5

pathway body : kidney->epo, epo->clearance, marrow->progenitors, progenitors->apoptosis, progenitors->red_cells, red_cells->spleen
